From e34b1efc93f1d50cabfb18a7c6a79ac703f1458d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 06:56:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Record per-site type feedback, item 4-1

The inline caches already observe shapes, but they observe them to answer the
current read: entries are replaced when stale (item 2-12) and dropped entirely
once a site passes four shapes. Feedback has to RETAIN, because "this site only
ever saw one shape" is a claim about history and a structure designed to be
overwritten cannot make it. TypeFeedback keeps, per site, the distinct receiver
shapes at a property read and the distinct callee identities at a call, plus
the observation count and whether the site overflowed the four-entry cap -- the
same threshold the cache calls megamorphic.

Two gates, and they are not the same gate. Property feedback is a runtime flag
tested inside the site helper, which already pays a predictable branch per read
for the cache-hit counter. Call feedback is gated at COMPILE time: with the
flag clear the compiler returns the call's target expression untouched, so the
emitted call is the one emitted before this existed -- no extra hop, no extra
branch, no extra argument. A call costs ~255 ns and is the path phase 4 exists
to fix; instrumenting it unconditionally in order to measure it would be
self-defeating. The cost is that enabling the flag does not retrofit compiled
code, which a test pins rather than leaving to be discovered.

The item buys no throughput -- 4-2 and 4-4 are what consume it -- so the
deliverable is what it says about the real corpus, which nothing in the engine
could report before. Seven Octane suites, weighted by executed operations
rather than by site count: 93.54% of 37.9M property reads and 96.70% of 4.24M
calls happen at a site that only ever saw one shape or one callee. Phase 4's
two XL items are well-founded on this corpus. Megamorphism is essentially
absent (18 sites in total, five of seven suites have none), which corroborates
2-10's finding of zero megamorphic read sites. DeltaBlue is the worst read case
at 77.10% with 43 polymorphic sites against Richards's 1 -- a lead on what is
left of the suite that still fails phase 2's gate.

Cost when off is zero by construction for calls, and for reads is below what
this container can resolve: six ABBA-interleaved pairs over a 60M-read loop
give a median paired ratio of 0.9835 with a 0.961-1.019 spread, so the change
arm came out nominally faster and the probe bounds the cost at about +/-2%
rather than showing one.

Adds --type-feedback, TypeFeedbackTests (16 cases), and leaves the item's third
signal -- numeric-vs-generic outcomes per site -- deliberately uncollected:
the compiler already proves numeric-ness statically, so a runtime counter would
have to be defined against that to say anything new.
